Summary:

The brief: "Customers of secure-startup.com have been receiving convincing phishing emails — can you figure out why?" The answer is in the domain's email-authentication DNS records.
A permissive SPF qualifier (?all, neutral) combined with a non-enforcing DMARC policy (p=none) means receivers won't reject forged mail, so the domain is trivially spoofable. The flag is split across the two TXT records.

Walkthrough:

1. SPF record
bash
dig secure-startup.com TXT
secure-startup.com. IN TXT "v=spf1 a mx ?all - HTB{RIP_SPF_Always_2nd"
v=spf1 a mx ?all authorizes the domain's A and MX hosts, but ?all is the neutral qualifier: everything else is neither passed nor failed. A neutral SPF result gives receivers no basis to reject, so SPF provides no real protection here.
The record also carries the first half of the flag.

2. DMARC record
bash
dig _dmarc.secure-startup.com TXT
_dmarc.secure-startup.com. IN TXT "v=DMARC1;p=none;_F1ddl3_2_DMARC}"
p=none is monitor-only: even when SPF/DKIM fail, receivers are told to take no action. With no enforcement above a neutral SPF, forged "From: @secure-startup.com" mail sails through.
The record carries the second half of the flag.

3. Combine:
