Root cause:
Email-authentication misconfiguration (no effective anti-spoofing). SPF with ?all expresses no policy on unlisted senders, and DMARC p=none enforces nothing — so neither mechanism stops a spoofed sender.
There is no DKIM signing in the records either. The result: the domain can be impersonated in phishing against its own customers.

Defensive takeaways:
Control	Weak (observed)	Hardened
SPF	?all (neutral)	-all (hard fail) once all legitimate senders are listed
DMARC	p=none	p=quarantine → p=reject after monitoring
DKIM	absent	sign outbound mail; publish the public key
Monitoring	none	set rua=/ruf= to receive DMARC aggregate/forensic reports

Move to enforcement gradually: start at p=none with rua reporting to find legitimate senders, then raise to quarantine and reject once SPF/DKIM alignment is confirmed, to avoid dropping valid mail.

Key lessons:
?all and p=none together mean "we authenticate nothing" — the combination, not either record alone, is what makes the domain spoofable.
Email-security OSINT lives in TXT records: SPF at the domain apex, DMARC at _dmarc.<domain>, DKIM at <selector>._domainkey.<domain>.
Here the flag was literally split across the SPF and DMARC records — always read the full TXT content, not just the policy tokens.

